  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\n\nAs an upper level shortwave approaches, a weak surface low will\nmove from eastern New York early this afternoon to the southern\nNew Hampshire/Maine border by the evening. These features will\nlead to increasing rain chances later this afternoon before\nexpanding north and east into western Maine through the first\npart of tonight. The highest chances (60 to 90 percent), will be\nalong the track of the low and trailing cold front, which \nshould move across southern New Hampshire and into portions of \nthe western Maine Foothills, the Interior, and the coast. CAMs \nhave come into fairly solid agreement on the track of heavier \nshowers and RAP soundings suggest that we could see some MUCAPE \naround 100 J/kg. Thus, there will be a convective element to \nthis precipitation which may lead to some pulsey embedded \ndownpours. A rumble of thunder or two can't be ruled out, \neither. Best guess is that we will see the higher totals in the\n0.5 to 1 inch range which matches well with what the 12z HREF \nmean is advertising, but HREF 24-hour max QPF does suggest the\npotential for some 1.5 to 2 inch totals. Thus, some locally \nhigher totals will be possible depending on how convection \nevolves. Rain will then move out quickly overnight as the low \nmoves into the Gulf of Maine.\n\nBehind the cold front, we should see plenty of sunshine and a\nstiff breeze on Friday. While pressure rises are not overly\nimpressive, we will see some decent cold air advection and steep\nlow-level lapse rates. Forecast NAM and GFS soundings suggest \nthat we will see 30 to 35 knots at the top of the mixed layer so\nwith the favorable lapse rates, widespread gusts in the 25 to 35\nmph range seem reasonable. The strongest winds appear to be most\nfavorable over Maine. Gusty winds could lead to a limited power\noutage threat from any branches that come down. Winds will\ndiminish rather quickly around and after sunset Friday evening,\nbecoming calm overnight. While we could still have some\nlingering high clouds around, we should have some areas that\nwill see favorable conditions for radiational cooling. \nOvernight lows may dip into the low to mid 30s along and north \nof the mountains, with values in the upper 30s to mid 40s \nelsewhere. Some areas of frost will be possible across the \nnorth.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\nHigh pressure slides through the region Saturday which will \nmake it the pick of the weekend. Upper level troughing drops 850\nmb temperatures to +2 to +5 C, so it is going to be on the \ncooler side with highs capping out in the low to mid 60s. Clouds\nincrease Saturday night as a shortwave moves through the \nbroader trough toward our area and high pressure begins to push \noffshore. Temperatures fall into the mid to upper 30s north of \nthe mountains, and into the low to mid-40s south.\n\nHigh pressure continues to stay nosed in through Sunday \nmorning, but rain moves into New Hampshire by Sunday afternoon \nas a wave of low pressure takes a track through southern New \nEngland. This creates some uncertainty in the northward extent \nof the precipitation shield, but forcing from the shortwave \nshould be enough to get measurable rainfall up through most of \nthe area. The higher rainfall totals are likely going to fall \nsouth of Concord where better forcing is going to overlap with \nhigher moisture. NBM is currently advertising probabilities of a\nhalf inch around 40 to 50 percent in that area. \n\nRain wraps up overnight and by late Monday morning most of the \narea should be back under mostly sunny skies. Global models are \nin good agreement that strong high pressure builds and then \nsettles over the region through most of next week. This brings \nmostly dry conditions with daytime temperatures in the 60s. \nNighttime temperatures are mostly going to be seasonable as well\n(ranging from the upper 30s in the north to 40s in the south), \nbut high pressure overhead usually allows for radiational \ncooling and I would not be surprised if we see frosts in \nnorthern zones more than once.",
